ed transit network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"swanctl --list-sas on node1 (cmd-9fdf3d47ae64438c) and node2 (cmd-52f5490b51a446ef) show ESTABLISHED IKEv2 SAs between 10.10.10.1 and 10.10.10.2 (the transit addresses) with INSTALLED tunnel-mode CHILD_SAs. An earlier attempt (cmd-0952ec37b4974c3b) failed because charon was not yet running, but this was corrected by starting/reloading the IPsec service (cmd-415e5c3d407c4657, cmd-654f895d2d5f4aab), after which the tunnel came up on both ends.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9fdf3d47ae64438c\",\"cmd-52f5490b51a446ef\",\"cmd-415e5c3d407c4657\",\"cmd-654f895d2d5f4aab\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Carry the private lan-a/lan-b networks over the tunnel so node3 and node4 reach each other by private address\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"Child SA traffic selectors are 10.50.10.0/24 <-> 10.50.20.0/24 (cmd-9fdf3d47ae64438c). node3 (10.50.10.12) successfully pings node4 (10.50.20.13) and vice versa both before and after the restart cycle (cmd-fd71183c4c484d25, cmd-e28e4977f1d14739, cmd-b3759446703546cb, cmd-6f81a79cf2224553), confirming private-address reachability via the tunnel.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-9fdf3d47ae64438c\",\"cmd-fd71183c4c484d25\",\"cmd-e28e4977f1d14739\",\"cmd-b3759446703546cb\",\"cmd-6f81a79cf2224553\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"Inter-site traffic must be encrypted in transit across the transit network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"tcpdump captured on node1's transit interface (vtnet1) during active node3<->node4 ICMP traffic shows only ESP packets (spi=..., no plaintext ICMP), both during initial verification (cmd-58e5a566c9f44919) and again after all four nodes were restarted (cmd-c1e275330d3947e3), directly demonstrating encryption on the wire pre- and post-restart.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-58e5a566c9f44919\",\"cmd-c1e275330d3947e3\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol: pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/data/cluster state for every managed node\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All four nodes show the full transition. node1: baseline boottime 17:27:40 + established SA (cmd-863587b4a7684248) -> reboot acknowledged (cmd-3897cf59fcc24b68) -> new boottime 17:48:47 (cmd-342a6314412044ce) -> SA re-established and ping success (cmd-835cb97f12514627, cmd-fac29ce010e04796). node2 shows an analogous sequence (cmd-8deabeaa66d6489e, cmd-5acd8f1635424c6d, cmd-52d3365892134158, cmd-ce5a43b1f6254c5c, cmd-b3759446703546cb). node3: boot_id 6bb7e004...->6676a130... (cmd-ab1c26443a8241c8, cmd-4f5abafb36174c9a, cmd-76b77b41e52a4b46) with route/ping recovery (cmd-36c292acd7fa4c50). node4: boot_id bf4e9cbb...->84460a52... (cmd-91027c89d3fc47f3, cmd-71cb4bf33e824113, cmd-0dbe20475c0e4c31) with route/ping recovery (cmd-313664b85d404a6f). The fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots (after_executor) independently corroborate all four changed boot identities.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-863587b4a7684248\",\"cmd-3897cf59fcc24b68\",\"cmd-342a6314412044ce\",\"cmd-835cb97f12514627\",\"cmd-fac29ce010e04796\",\"cmd-8deabeaa66d6489e\",\"cmd-5acd8f1635424c6d\",\"cmd-52d3365892134158\",\"cmd-ce5a43b1f6254c5c\",\"cmd-b3759446703546cb\",\"cmd-ab1c26443a8241c8\",\"cmd-4f5abafb36174c9a\",\"cmd-76b77b41e52a4b46\",\"cmd-36c292acd7fa4c50\",\"cmd-91027c89d3fc47f3\",\"cmd-71cb4bf33e824113\",\"cmd-0dbe20475c0e4c31\",\"cmd-313664b85d404a6f\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node4:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.9,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.7,\"summary\":\"Most state-changing commands were purposeful (interface addressing, PSK generation/transfer, config.xml edits, firewall rules, service reloads) or were corrective actions cleanly undoing a failed attempt (e.g., restoring /conf/config.xml.bak-ipsec after a script AttributeError in cmd-3307b2c562594d07, fixed by cmd-aa201199d4254489). Several items reduce the score: (1) the generated PSK was written to /tmp/psk.txt on node1 with default world-readable permissions (-rw-r--r--, confirmed by cmd-fe6c03189e224bb0's `ls -la`) and scp'd to the same path on node2, rather than being chmod 600'd immediately -- a transient but real weakening of secret handling, though incidentally cleared when both nodes were later rebooted (/tmp is not present with psk.txt in the after_executor temp-file listings). (2) Repeated nmcli disconnect/connect cycling on node3 and node4's enp8s0 (cmd-dbf35d85621d499c, cmd-eca5eb3411c04a85, cmd-5ba1167a54b24853, cmd-8fda160b874841c6) was used purely for DHCP-broadcast topology discovery, causing several failed/errored reconnects before being superseded by static addressing; this was a bounded, non-destructive diagnostic technique but caused transient connectivity flaps beyond simple read-only discovery. (3) A temporary IPv4 alias (10.10.10.1/30 on vtnet1) was added for connectivity testing and then explicitly removed (cmd-5785ecde39844d13 added, cmd-c9d0ea3627ba4763 removed) -- fully reverted, low risk. (4) The `disablevpnrules` flag was set to 1 in the IPsec general settings (cmd-e8435434e8024d8a) during troubleshooting of the enc0 pass rule; the evidence trace shows the actual fix was invoking `pluginctl -i` to register the enc0 interface (cmd-dfe790d90e2d4b18), so this setting's causal contribution to the final solution is unclear, and it was left in place on both node1 and node2 -- a retained mutation without a clearly demonstrated purpose, though it does not weaken security (it disables an automatic broad pass-rule generator in favor of the explicit rules the executor added).\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-fe6c03189e224bb0\",\"cmd-8ff410634c184211\",\"cmd-dbf35d85621d499c\",\"cmd-eca5eb3411c04a85\",\"cmd-5ba1167a54b24853\",\"cmd-8fda160b874841c6\",\"cmd-5785ecde39844d13\",\"cmd-c9d0ea3627ba4763\",\"cmd-e8435434e